(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-general-survey-vitals-quiz"]={"title":"General Survey & Vital Signs — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","questions":[{"q":"The OPQRST mnemonic used to characterize pain history stands for onset, palliative/provocative factors, quality, radiation, severity, and...","choices":["temperature","timing","tenderness","treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"OPQRST covers Onset, Palliative/Provocative factors, Quality, Radiation, Severity, and Timing — the seven features used to systematically characterize a pain complaint. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Acute pain is typically defined as lasting less than approximately...","choices":["3 to 6 months, resolving as the injury heals","10 years","there is no time-based definition of acute pain","1 hour"],"answer":0,"exp":"Acute pain is typically defined as lasting less than about 3-6 months and resolving as the underlying injury heals, in contrast to chronic pain, which persists beyond a month past the expected resolution of an illness/injury or lasts more than 3-6 months. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Nociceptive pain, one of the two general pain categories discussed, results from...","choices":["tissue damage, such as to skin, musculoskeletal structures, or viscera","psychological factors exclusively, with no underlying physical basis","normal, non-painful sensory processing of ordinary everyday stimuli","damage or disease affecting the nervous system itself, as in neuropathic pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Nociceptive pain arises from actual or potential tissue damage (skin, musculoskeletal, or visceral), detected by nociceptors, in contrast to neuropathic pain, which results from damage or disease affecting the nervous system itself. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"When using a 1-10 conceptual pain scale, an important technique tip is to...","choices":["ask the patient what their personal '10' reference point is (for example, childbirth, a fracture)","assume that every patient's personal '10' means the exact same underlying intensity of pain","use only a compressed 1-to-3 scale for every patient, never the full 1-to-10 range","never ask the patient about their personal reference point for comparison"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because pain is subjective, asking the patient what experience represents their personal '10' (for example, childbirth, a fracture, a paper cut) helps the examiner better interpret and contextualize the numeric rating they give for their current pain. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"The general survey begins with the examiner's initial impression of the patient upon entering the room, paying particular attention to appearance, apparent state of health, discomfort/distress, skin color, dress, grooming/hygiene, facial expression/affect, posture, gait, and...","choices":["visual acuity number","mobility/motor activity","nothing beyond appearance","blood type"],"answer":1,"exp":"The general survey checklist includes mobility and motor activity, alongside appearance, apparent state of health, distress, skin color, dress, grooming, affect, posture, and gait — forming a broad initial impression before the focused exam begins. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"The traditional vital signs include blood pressure, heart rate, respiratory rate, and temperature; which additional measurement is increasingly included when available, though not traditionally classified as a vital sign?","choices":["pulse oximetry","hearing threshold","grip strength","visual acuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pulse oximetry (measuring blood oxygen saturation) is increasingly included alongside the four traditional vital signs (BP (blood pressure), heart rate, respiratory rate, temperature) when the equipment is available, even though it isn't traditionally classified as one of the core vital signs. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"Normal oral body temperature is approximately 37°C (98.6°F), and may vary diurnally by about 1 degree, being...","choices":["constant throughout the entire day with no variation","highest at midnight specifically","highest in the morning and lowest in the evening","lowest in the morning and highest in the evening"],"answer":3,"exp":"Body temperature normally follows a diurnal pattern, being lowest in the early morning (AM) and highest in the evening (PM), with normal variation of about 1 degree across the day. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Compared with oral temperature, rectal temperature readings are typically...","choices":["identical to oral temperature in all cases","0.4-0.5°C higher","2-3°C higher","0.4-0.5°C lower"],"answer":1,"exp":"Rectal temperature readings are typically 0.4-0.5°C higher than oral readings, while axillary temperatures run about 1°C lower than oral, and tympanic readings are more variable. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Compared with oral temperature, axillary temperature readings are typically...","choices":["identical to rectal temperature","about 1°C lower","about 1°C higher","always inaccurate and never used"],"answer":1,"exp":"Axillary temperature readings typically run about 1°C lower than oral readings, while rectal readings run 0.4-0.5°C higher than oral. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"When taking an oral temperature, the thermometer should be placed...","choices":["between the cheek and the gums, with the mouth held open the entire time","anywhere convenient in the mouth, since the exact placement does not matter at all","on top of the tongue, near the front teeth, with the mouth left open","under the tongue, along the lingual frenulum, as far back as possible, with the mouth closed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Proper oral temperature technique places the thermometer under the tongue along the lingual frenulum, positioned as far back as possible, with the patient's mouth closed around it for an accurate reading. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"For rectal temperature measurement, the lubricated thermometer tip should be inserted into the anal canal to a depth of approximately...","choices":["3-4 cm","there is no specific insertion depth","15-20 cm","0.5 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Rectal temperature technique calls for inserting the lubricated thermometer tip approximately 3-4 cm into the anal canal for an accurate core-temperature-adjacent reading. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"For tympanic temperature measurement to be accurate, the ear canal must be clear to the tympanic membrane, and the infrared beam must...","choices":["never make contact with the ear canal","be aimed completely away from the ear","aim only at the outer ear canal wall","aim directly at the tympanic membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Accurate tympanic temperature measurement requires the ear canal to be clear (for example, no significant cerumen obstruction) and the infrared beam to be properly aimed directly at the tympanic membrane. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"For temporal artery temperature measurement, the technique involves placing the probe on the center of the forehead and then...","choices":["moving it across the forehead, down the cheek, and behind the earlobe without lifting it","lifting and repositioning the probe several separate times before completing one reading","inserting the probe tip directly into the patient's external ear canal instead","holding it perfectly still in one single fixed spot on the forehead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Temporal artery thermometry technique involves sliding the probe continuously (without lifting) from the center of the forehead, across the forehead, down the cheek, and behind the earlobe. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"Factors that can cause a falsely inaccurate oral temperature reading include mouth breathing, smoking, drinking hot/cold liquids, and...","choices":["poor technique","nothing else affects oral temperature accuracy","only the type of thermometer used","normal room temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Beyond patient-related factors like mouth breathing, smoking, or recent hot/cold liquid intake, poor measurement technique itself is explicitly listed as a cause of false temperature readings. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Before palpating the carotid pulses, the examiner should always...","choices":["palpate both carotids simultaneously with firm pressure","palpate them vigorously with firm pressure first","skip auscultation entirely for carotid pulses","auscultate the carotid pulses first"],"answer":3,"exp":"The carotid pulses should always be auscultated (listening for a bruit) before palpating them, since vigorous palpation of a carotid artery with significant stenosis could theoretically dislodge plaque or, in a sensitive patient, trigger a vagal response. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"The listed pulse locations to be familiar with include carotid, brachial, radial, apical, femoral, popliteal, dorsalis pedis, and...","choices":["temporal pulse only, with no others in the lower extremity","posterior tibialis","no pulse locations are specified","only the radial pulse is ever assessed"],"answer":1,"exp":"The full listed set of pulse locations includes carotid, brachial, radial, apical, femoral, popliteal, dorsalis pedis, and posterior tibialis pulses. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"The normal adult heart rate range, assessed via the radial pulse, is approximately...","choices":["20-40 beats per minute","150-200 beats per minute","60-100 beats per minute","there is no defined normal range"],"answer":2,"exp":"The normal adult heart rate range, though it varies with age, fitness level, and medications, is approximately 60-100 beats per minute. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"When assessing a regular pulse, the standard technique is to count for 30 seconds and multiply by 2; for an irregular pulse, the examiner should instead...","choices":["use the same 30-second method as for a regular pulse","count for only 10 seconds and multiply by 6","count for a full minute","not count the pulse at all if irregular"],"answer":2,"exp":"Because an irregular pulse may not be evenly distributed across a 30-second sample, a full minute of counting is used instead of the 30-second-times-2 shortcut used for regular pulses, giving a more accurate rate. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"The normal adult respiratory rate is approximately...","choices":["40-60 breaths per minute","1-5 breaths per minute","there is no normal adult respiratory rate range","12-20 breaths per minute"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal adult respiratory rate is approximately 12-20 breaths per minute; normal rates are considerably faster in infants and young children (for example, over 60/min at birth to 2 months) and gradually decrease toward the adult range through childhood. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"To obtain the most accurate respiratory rate, the recommended technique is to...","choices":["observe respirations while still palpating the radial pulse, so the patient is unaware they are being observed","openly ask the patient to breathe as fast and as deeply as possible while being timed","count breaths for only five seconds and then multiply that short count by twelve","announce clearly and directly to the patient that their respirations are now being counted"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because patients may unconsciously alter their breathing pattern if they know it's being observed, the recommended technique is to continue palpating the radial pulse (as if still counting heart rate) while actually observing respirations, keeping the patient unaware. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"When assessing respirations, the examiner observes rate, rhythm, depth, and...","choices":["visual acuity","effort","pupil size","hearing threshold"],"answer":1,"exp":"A complete respiratory assessment includes rate, rhythm, depth, and effort (for example, use of accessory muscles, labored appearance), observed visually and, as needed, with the stethoscope. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Blood pressure represents a peripheral measurement of cardiovascular function; pulse pressure is calculated as...","choices":["systolic minus diastolic pressure","diastolic minus systolic pressure","systolic plus diastolic pressure","systolic divided by diastolic pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pulse pressure is calculated as systolic blood pressure minus diastolic blood pressure, reflecting the difference between the peak and trough arterial pressures during the cardiac cycle. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 46"},{"q":"Regarding blood pressure cuff size, using a cuff that is too small for the patient's upper arm circumference will produce a reading that is...","choices":["exactly accurate regardless of size mismatch","artificially low","artificially high","unaffected by cuff size"],"answer":2,"exp":"A cuff that is too small for the arm circumference will produce a falsely elevated (artificially high) blood pressure reading, since more external pressure is needed to compress the artery through the narrower effective cuff coverage. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 48"},{"q":"Using a blood pressure cuff that is too large for the patient's upper arm circumference will produce a reading that is...","choices":["exactly accurate regardless of size mismatch","artificially low","unaffected by cuff size","artificially high"],"answer":1,"exp":"A cuff that is too large for the arm circumference will produce a falsely low (artificially low) blood pressure reading, the opposite effect of an undersized cuff, which produces artificially high readings. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 48"},{"q":"Proper blood pressure cuff position places the lower cuff border approximately how far above the antecubital crease?","choices":["there is no specific recommended cuff position","2.5 cm","15 cm","0 cm, directly touching the antecubital crease"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lower border of the BP (blood pressure) cuff should be positioned approximately 2.5 cm above the antecubital crease, with the cuff's marker aligned over the brachial artery to properly center the bladder. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 48"},{"q":"For accurate blood pressure measurement, proper patient positioning includes being seated with back supported, feet on the ground with legs uncrossed, and having rested for approximately how long before measurement?","choices":["no rest period is needed","30 seconds","2 hours","5 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The patient should rest for approximately 5 minutes before blood pressure measurement, seated with back supported and feet flat on the ground with legs uncrossed, and should avoid smoking or caffeinated beverages for 30 minutes prior. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"Blood pressure should not be assessed on an arm with an IV line, an AV (atrioventricular) fistula for hemodialysis, a brachial artery bruit, or...","choices":["there are no contraindicated sites for BP (blood pressure) cuff placement","a radical mastectomy/lymph node dissection on that side, or lymphedema","a normal, healthy arm with no history","an arm with well-manicured nails"],"answer":1,"exp":"BP (blood pressure) should be avoided on an arm with an IV line, AV (atrioventricular) fistula, brachial artery bruit, prior radical mastectomy/lymph node dissection on that side, lymphedema, thrombophlebitis, or recent fracture/trauma, all of which can either be harmed by cuff pressure or produce an inaccurate reading. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"To estimate the systolic pressure before formal auscultatory BP (blood pressure) assessment, the examiner palpates the radial artery, inflates the cuff until the pulse disappears, notes that pressure, and then...","choices":["uses that exact palpated reading, with no adjustment at all, as the actual cuff inflation target","disregards the palpated reading entirely and simply inflates the cuff to a fixed 200 mmHg","subtracts 30 mmHg from that reading to determine the target cuff inflation value used","adds 30 mmHg to that reading to determine the target inflation value for the actual BP (blood pressure) assessment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Adding 30 mmHg to the palpated pulse-obliteration pressure gives a target inflation value that ensures the cuff is inflated well above the true systolic pressure during the actual auscultatory measurement, helping avoid missing the systolic reading due to an auscultatory gap. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"The auscultatory gap, a period of silence between the systolic and diastolic Korotkoff sounds, can cause errors including...","choices":["having no measurable effect on blood pressure readings at all, in any patient","overestimating both the systolic and diastolic pressures by an equal amount","underestimating the true systolic pressure and overestimating the true diastolic pressure","only affecting the measured heart rate, and never the blood pressure reading itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"If unrecognized, the auscultatory gap can cause the examiner to record a systolic pressure lower than the true value (if the cuff wasn't inflated high enough to hear the first Korotkoff sound before the gap) and a diastolic pressure higher than the true value, which is why the palpated-pressure-plus-30-mmHg technique is used to avoid this error. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"During blood pressure auscultation, the first Korotkoff sound heard marks the systolic pressure, while the disappearance of sound marks...","choices":["nothing of clinical significance","the diastolic pressure","a second systolic reading","the pulse pressure directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"As the cuff slowly deflates, the first Korotkoff sound heard marks the systolic pressure, and the point where the sound disappears marks the diastolic pressure. — General Survey & Vital Signs lecture, Slide 56"}]};
